/** Translation maps for siman 050 — imported by _patch-siman-050.mjs */
export const TRANSLATIONS = {
  mechaber: {
    '1#main': `General laws regarding perforation of organs. In it are 3 seifim. For all these perforations, if there is something to hang upon — such as the butcher or a wolf that took them — we hang upon them. If there is nothing to hang upon and there is doubt whether it was perforated while alive or not — we pierce a perforation next to it and compare this to that; and even if they are not similar, if they used by hand on the doubtful perforation they also use on the perforation they make to compare with it; if after use it is kosher — kosher in its place. And one compares only in the kereis and dekin, but not in the heart, spleen, and gizzard. {Rama: And see above siman 36 how we practice; every place where one cannot hang that it was done after slaughter — even though it is a treifah that has inspection, if they did not inspect it or cannot inspect it — behold it is like a safek treifah and we forbid it; and we do not say "she was slaughtered, she is permitted" except regarding a matter where we can say the defect was done after slaughter, that she did not leave her presumption of life.}`,
    '2#main': `Every organ regarding which we said that if it is perforated in any amount it is treifah — likewise if all of it was removed — treifah; whether removed through illness or by hand, or created missing, or created with two organs of that organ — treifah, for all excess is considered as removed. {Rama: (except the spleen, which if removed is kosher, and if perforated in its support is treifah) (Tur); and everything we said that if created missing it is treifah — all the more so if removed by hand; and everything we said that if removed it is treifah — the same law applies to created missing. And some permit in this (and in great loss one may rely on those who permit) (darkhei emet).}`,
    '3#main': `Every organ whose perforation or deficiency or removal disqualifies — if it dissolved — disqualified. {Rama: And we, who are not expert — any place where the appearance of the flesh changed for the worse, behold it is as perforated there (Hagahot Maimonides and Mordechai).}`,
  },
  'siftei-kohen': {
    '1#א': `["<b>And see above siman 36.</b> Seif 5 was explained that we are not expert in hakafah — see there:"]`,
    '1#ב': `["<b>Every place, etc.</b> This is a standalone matter and requires further study; and see in Taz who mixed the matters:"]`,
    '1#ג': `["<b>Every place, etc.</b> Thus wrote Darkei Moshe at the end of siman 41: an incident came before us in Krakow in the head of an animal where there was water, and before they knew the defect they cut the skull in two and part of the brain from the skull, and it was not known whether the brain surrounded the water or not, and we forbade it; and some wanted to permit from that which is taught: 'she was slaughtered, she is permitted' until you know what made her treifah — and this is not comparable, since here there is a clear defect from life, one may not permit for this reason; and so is implied in Tosafot chapter Four Brothers (Yevamot daf 31), end of his words. And according to these words are his words in the hagahah here, and Maharam of Lublin in his responsum siman 66 challenged the Rav's words and said: on the contrary, Tosafot there rejected this distinction and Tosafot's conclusion is that even where the defect was done from life, nevertheless one may establish it in a presumption of permissibility, as we say: 'this woman stands in a presumption of permissibility for the marketplace — do not forbid her from doubt,' and that which we are concerned for safek drusah and similarly above siman 33, a thorn sat in the esophagus — we are concerned lest it healed — this is because it is common. And that which Rashba wrote in his responsum siman 283 and 300, that when a needle was found stuck and there was no time to check whether it perforated from both sides — treifah — this too is because it is common; and likewise that which we practice to forbid water in the brain is also because it is common — see there where he elaborated in explaining these matters and in resolving Rashba's words, and wrote: 'I was forced to explain Rashba's words thus so that Tosafot's difficulty from above in chapter Four Brothers should not challenge his words,' end of his words in brief. And in truth one who examines Tosafot there will see as Maharam said; but it appears to me that the Rav's view is that Tosafot wrote thus for Rabbah who said in the Gemara there 'this woman stands in a presumption of permissibility,' etc.; but Abaye challenged Rabbah there from that which a house fell on him, etc. — why not say 'this woman stands in a presumption of permissibility,' etc.; and Abaye and Rava conclude that we do not establish 'this woman' in her presumption — if so, Tosafot who compare this law to 'she was slaughtered, she is permitted' prove that where we do not establish 'this woman' in a presumption of permissibility, likewise we do not say 'she was slaughtered, she is permitted' where a defect was born from life; and even though it is possible to say that for the conclusion too we say 'this woman stands in a presumption of permissibility,' only that for stringency we say she requires chalitzah, as Beit Yosef wrote there — and also in Tosafot's words it is somewhat strained to say they did not write thus for the conclusion; nevertheless, since it is not compelled and there is room here and there, and also the simple meaning of the Gemara there implies that Abaye argues on the essence of the law — if so, from where do we learn permissibility; perhaps the conclusion stands that we do not establish 'this woman' in her presumption — so it appears to me according to the Rav. And this that he wrote 'and so is implied in Tosafot chapter Four Brothers' means that they wrote there initially this distinction, and even though they rejected it there afterward — that was for Rabbah; but for Abaye and Rava, whom we rule like, that distinction may be true and stand. And for practical law it requires further study; for that which Maharam strained in Rashba's words is certainly not so — and it escaped him (and also the Rav) Rashba's words in his novellae on the first chapter of Chullin, who wrote: 'and where the bird's wing slipped off,' etc. '(and safek before slaughter we go stringently)' until we hang on a wolf — so wrote our Rabbi, may his memory be blessed, end of Rashba; and so wrote Ran there — all these matters; and he wrote that so explained Rav R' Yonah, may his memory be blessed; and that which Rashba said 'this entire safek in slaughter is invalid' — the same applies to all other treifot — namely, certainly every Torah safek is stringently; only that in the matter of 'every' it expanded safek in slaughter that we establish on a presumption of prohibition even where it is not common — and requires further study. And so is proven from Semak end of siman 200, daf 93b, who wrote: 'and every safek treifah that comes before a sage and is not clarified — such as the bone broke and emerged outside, or safek sircha, or any other safek where he does not know if they are before slaughter or after slaughter, and likewise buka d'atma where he scraped from its place and does not know if its nivin were eaten or not — in all of them Hagahot Gedolot ruled to prohibit and we do not say "she was slaughtered, she is permitted" as we say regarding a wolf that came and took the intestinal children,' etc. 'there there are some legs to the matter that it was done after slaughter; but where there are no legs to the matter at all we do not hang,' end of his words; and so Or Zarua general rule 50 din 5: 'if there is doubt whether its nivin were eaten or it was found after slaughter — safek in the meeting of sinews and all such cases — Hagahot Gedolot and Semak wrote that one must be stringent in its doubt and we do not say "she was slaughtered, she is permitted" except where there is something to hang on visible permissibility, such as a wolf that took the intestinal children before us and returned them perforated,' end of Or Zarua. Thus, wherever there is nothing to hang on neither for permissibility nor for prohibition — as in safek whether its nivin were eaten, and meeting of sinews, and such — we hang stringently and one may not conclude from Maharam of Lublin's responsum that from Semak and Or Zarua it implies we do not hang stringently except where the prohibition is common, per Tosafot's view above — and this is certainly not so. But from Semak and Semag siman 63 daf 143b and other poskim who wrote regarding a broken bone and he does not know if from life or after death, or dislocated thigh and he does not know if its nivin were eaten — that one must forbid — there is no comparison to the wolf, for there there are legs to the matter, unlike here where the doubts depend on lack of expertise. And so Maharai in Hagahot Sha'ar HaDin end of chapter 7 explained: wherever there is something to hang on for permissibility and prohibition, we hang leniently because of 'she was slaughtered stands in a presumption of permissibility until it is known,' etc., per Tosafot chapter Four Brothers; and all those poskim are fit to rely upon in great loss; and similarly. But without great loss one should rule like R' Yonah, Rashba, and Ran above — that where prohibition and permissibility are equal we hang stringently even where the defect was born only after slaughter; and even though it seems that what the poskim meant by 'safek of lack of expertise' is because there is nothing to hang on permissibility more than prohibition — and so is explained in responsa Masat Binyamin siman 50 daf 87b; and so in Or Zarua — nevertheless the simple meaning of the poskim does not imply thus, and likewise in Maharai's words there it implies that the main distinction is because it is lack of expertise — see there and requires further study. And likewise even where a defect was born from life, there is room to hang leniently in great loss where the prohibition is not more common than permissibility, per Maharam's responsum — for so appears the simple meaning of Tosafot Pesachim (daf 43a) s.v. k'savar, etc., and so is written in Pesakim Tosafot there: 'a safek was born from life and she is still in a presumption of prohibition — nevertheless "she was slaughtered, she is permitted,"' end of his words. And nevertheless one must be stringent in this per the Rav's words in the hagahah and above siman 53 s.k. 13, and where permissibility is more common we hang even from life leniently, as is proven from that of Shegruna — and so is proven from the poskim below siman 52 s.k. 14:"]`,
    '2#א': `["<b>Except the spleen.</b> And even though above siman 45 some are stringent regarding perforation — whether treifah if removed is disputed, we are not concerned:"]`,
    '2#ב': `["<b>And some permit, etc.</b> See above siman 54 seif 3 and what is written there:"]`,
    '3#_': `If it dissolved, etc. See above siman 43 seif 2 in the hagahah and siman 48 seif 5:`,
  },
  'turei-zahav': {
    '1#_': `But not in the heart. Tur wrote "in the liver and heart," and Beit Yosef wondered — behold perforation does not disqualify the liver; and he answered that it deals with the liver stalk, and Maharshal explained that it deals with the liver opposite the gallbladder — namely, the gallbladder was perforated and the place of the liver opposite it was also perforated; then it is forbidden, as above siman 42:`,
    '2#א': `Whether removed through illness, etc., or created missing. Regarding perforation he did not need to write thus, for everything created with a perforation has no name of perforation but deficiency, and it is included in removal — as written at the beginning of siman 43, see there:`,
    '2#ב': `And some permit. This is Rambam's view, who holds that if you say that also created missing is treifah where they said removal is treifah — it is one matter, removed and deficient; and why did they count them as two. And the first view, which is Rashba's view, holds that they are certainly equal and both were written so as not to give anyone room to err and distinguish:`,
    '3#_': `Every place where appearance changed, etc. Maharshal wrote: specifically where the flesh changed from its appearance; but when no change is seen in the flesh one need not be concerned even if the flesh tore to say perhaps it dissolved and it is as removal — only we do not establish a defect, end of his words:`,
  },
  'baer-heitev': {
    '1#א': `In the heart. Taz wrote that in Tur it says "in the liver and heart," and Beit Yosef wondered — behold perforation does not disqualify the liver; and he answered that it deals with the liver stalk; and Maharshal explained that it deals with the liver opposite the gallbladder — namely the gallbladder was perforated and the place of the liver opposite it was also perforated; then it is forbidden, as above siman 45:`,
    '1#ב': `Practice. There in seif 5 it was explained that we are not expert in hakafah:`,
    '1#ג': `She was slaughtered. Shach wrote that Maharam of Lublin in his responsa disagrees in this law and brought proof from Tosafot chapter Four Brothers; and he settles Rama's words; and for practical law Shach ruled, and also Taz at the end of siman 48, that we do not say "she was slaughtered, she is permitted" except where there is something to hang on visible permissibility — such as a wolf came and took the intestinal children before us and returned them perforated; but where there is nothing to hang on neither for permissibility nor for prohibition — such as safek in the meeting of sinews or its nivin were eaten, and such — we hang stringently and do not say "she was slaughtered, she is permitted," only specifically where there are legs to the matter that it was done after slaughter; and where permissibility is more common we hang on it even from life (and see Peri Chadash siman 29 s.k. 1). And proof from Shegruna at the end of siman 32. And several poskim hold that in great loss one may be lenient wherever there is something to hang on for permissibility and prohibition because of "she was slaughtered, she is permitted" — if not a safek due to lack of expertise — and see above end of siman 55:`,
    '2#א': `Perforated. Shach wrote: and even though above siman 45 some are stringent regarding perforation — whether kosher if removed is disputed — we are not concerned:`,
    '2#ב': `To created missing. (The same.) If created perforated. A goose whose skin was whole on its entire outside and a deep depression was seen two finger-widths wide in the middle of the belly along its length, and it was evident there was a deficiency inside under the skin; they skinned it and found that the middle bone protruding in the middle like a thin board — the flesh on both sides was cracked and split along its entire length and in its very middle; and in that break between them the heart and liver were seen from outside as they lie in the body cavity, and it appeared to have been done thus from birth — in Sefer Tzitzat Tzvi siman 76 he leaned to permit; but because it was a new matter not seen like it by the multitude of treifah inspectors, see there. And see in Peri Chadash s.k. 2 what he challenged on him. See below siman 54 in Shach s.k. 9:`,
    '3#א': `Dissolved. See above siman 43 seif 2 in the hagahah and siman 48 seif 5:`,
    '3#ב': `That changed. Maharshal wrote: specifically where the flesh changed from its appearance; but when no change is seen in the flesh one need not be concerned even if the flesh tore to say perhaps it dissolved and it is as removal — only we do not establish a defect, end of his words:`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#_': `Conclusion of the Gemara per Rav Ada bar Ahavah — Chullin daf 49:`,
    '2#_': `As Rav Huna explained there on daf 9:`,
    '3#_': `Statement of Rav Shimi bar Chiya there on daf 50:`,
  },
  'beur-hagra': {
    '1#א': `Or in a wolf. There; and see in Hagahot Gedolot:`,
    '1#ב': `But not, etc. For they are not at all in the category of intestinal children in the language of the Gemara — as stated above 51b: Amemar said, etc.; Mar Zutra said to him, etc.; and he adds even against the walls such as heart and spleen, etc.; and such as according to Amemar one needs only against the kereis and dekin:`,
    '1#ג': `See above, etc. Meaning that we are not expert:`,
    '1#ד': `Every place where one cannot, etc. Shach. And Peri Chadash challenged and agreed with Maharam of Lublin; and see above siman 39 s.k. 16 and 17, and likewise there seif 5. And the upshot: we forbid only regarding a common matter, as Tosafot wrote there; and with this what is written 51a — that in all perforations of the world we declare treifah even without hakafah even though there is room to hang after slaughter (and see above end of siman 55 and end of siman 56): (Likkut) Every place, etc. And so in Semak end of the five treifot and in Tur (end).`,
    '2#א': `Every organ, etc. As stated above siman 40 seif 5: (Likkut) Every, etc. In Tur; and it is not comparable to the spleen, for there we say (55b) and the halacha is not, etc., only specifically in its support and nothing remained, etc. Tosafot and Rosh end of chapter 8. And Ra'avyah disagrees — Hagahot Ashri there (end): (Likkut) Every organ, etc. From that which is stated 58b: that animal, etc., even though we did not teach regarding them except perforation — and this is why "the spleen was removed" is kosher specifically regarding the spleen (end): (Likkut) Every organ, etc. In Tur; and even though regarding the spleen, etc. — it is different there, for its perforation is not like other perforations, for we distinguish between its stalk and its support; and if something remained, etc. Rosh. And Ran proved from that which is taught "the spleen was removed" — and if so, why is the spleen singled out; and likewise in chapter 4 "one cuts," etc. — only specifically the spleen; and so as above. And Ra'avyah disagrees — see Mordechai and Hagahot Ashri there (end):`,
    '2#ב': `And likewise if, etc. As is written 58b:`,
    '2#ג': `Except, etc. 55a:`,
    '2#ד': `And everything, etc. There:`,
    '2#ה': `And everything, etc. The same law, etc. From that which is stated: an olive-bulk in place of gall — and therefore it must be said because of gall; and if so, the same applies to created missing; and the mishnah taught me regarding liver that was removed:`,
    '2#ו': `And some, etc. Hagahot Gedolot:`,
    '3#_': `Every, etc. 53b: we see, etc.:`,
  },
  kereti: {
    '1#א': `And we compare this to that — and we are not expert in inspection; and it is possible to be stringent — it appears so, and requires further study:`,
    '1#ב': `And we do not say "she was slaughtered" — see Peleti where I elaborated on this and raised whether a defect from life is forbidden in any case rabbinically and we do not rely on her presumption at all — see there at length:`,
    '1#ג': `Except regarding a matter, etc. Shach and acharonim — their views do not agree on this, only if the prohibition is more common and there are legs to the matter that it was done after slaughter; but a balanced safek — safek d'oraisa stringently — and we forbid it; and see Peleti where I agreed with Rama without swaying from his words right or left; and on the contrary, in a balanced safek we say to be lenient only here where there are legs to the matter that it was done from life. And I clarified that Rambam also holds thus, and we have only Rama's words:`,
    '2#א': `Whether created missing or created perforated — and see above regarding the spleen that Tzitzat Tzvi's view is to distinguish between a perforation that has a cavity and a perforation on the surface of something flat where "created perforated" does not apply — and see what I wrote on this:`,
    '2#ב': `From the spleen — and the same applies to the lung: if removed — kosher, and if perforated — some views are stringent and declare treifah — Shach:`,
    '2#ג': `One may rely on those who permit; and Peri Chadash wrote one should not be lenient at all; and see Peleti where I rejected all his claims and brought proof for Rambam and that Tosafot also hold like Rambam — see there:`,
    '3#_': `Every place where appearance changed — there must be a complete change and somewhat recognizable decay; but with a partial change in appearance one certainly need not be concerned. Also the change must be from side to side — otherwise it is not considered dissolved; nevertheless we do not declare treifah — and it is simple that the Torah spared Israel's money. Heaven forbid to be stringent where there is no doubt:`,
  },
  peleti: {
    '1#_': `Every place where one cannot hang that it was done after slaughter, etc. — behold this is safek treifah and we do not say "she was slaughtered," etc.; and see Shach s.k. 3 who elaborated on this; and likewise Peri Chadash at the beginning of siman 29 elaborated on this matter. And to clarify all this briefly: they said in the Gemara Yevamot chapter 3: and all of them where there was doubt of kiddushin — close to him, close to her; and likewise safek divorce — chalitzah and not yibbum; and similarly — why did it not teach regarding divorce "close to him," etc.; and Rabbah answers: because the end teaches "this woman stands in a presumption of permissibility for the marketplace" — and you come to forbid her from doubt of divorce; we establish her presumption and she is permitted, and we do not say to be stringent to require chalitzah: if you say chalitzah, yibbum; unlike kiddushin — "this woman stands in a presumption of permissibility for yibbum," and we come to forbid her from doubt of kiddushin — we are stringent to require chalitzah. And what do you care if you say chalitzah, yibbum — she is permitted to yibbum by law in any case. Tosafot challenged on "this woman," etc., regarding safek drusah — why are we concerned; behold the animal has a presumption of permissibility; and if a defect was born from life — here too a defect from life; and nevertheless we go within the presumption. And they answer: drusah is common; and likewise "a thorn sat in the esophagus" — see there; and so Tosafot Chullin 43b s.v. k'savar. And behold the Darkei Moshe wrote in siman 41: an incident in Krakow in the brain where there was water and it was not known if the brain surrounded the water or not, and the sages of Krakow forbade — since a defect from life, we do not say "she was slaughtered, she is permitted," as Tosafot wrote in Yevamot. And Maharam of Lublin wondered — he brings a fault upon himself, for in Tosafot it is mentioned in the explanation of that which "close to him, close to her" in yibbum — safek close to him, close to her proves we rely on presumption even though a defect was born from life. However Shach wrote: it may be said they do not argue on the essence of the reasoning, only they thought to be stringent to require chalitzah and we do not say "if you say chalitzah, yibbum" — but they do not argue on the essence of the law that there is no presumption. And Rav Peri Chadash proved that otherwise what does Tosafot challenge from safek drusah — perhaps this baraita was taught after the conclusion that there is no presumption, since a defect from life. And it appears for practical law Rama's words are compelled — for in the Gemara they said there: Abaye said "his neighbor will testify about him," the tanna taught in Kiddushin, and the same for divorce — it is difficult how the mishnah was left undifferentiated and taught in Kiddushin and leaned to learn for divorce — behold the subjects are not equal in kiddushin; one may well say they were stringent for chalitzah; and if there is concern she will do yibbum thereby the law stands that she is permitted to yibbum, unlike divorce — how can we be stringent for chalitzah lest she do yibbum and stumble in a Torah prohibition; and even though in truth we are not concerned for this — if she does chalitzah, yibbum — nevertheless this reasoning exists; and how did the mishnah rely on this to say this comes in kiddushin to learn from it for divorce — behold their matters differ; and with this if she does yibbum it is not a prohibition, and with this she stumbles in ervah — therefore it must be said for the conclusion there is no presumption and it deteriorated from life; and if so, divorce and kiddushin are equal and one may well learn this from that. And this is correct and straight. And what Peri Chadash argued — if so what does Tosafot challenge from safek drusah; for practical law it is clear there in Chullin: Rabbah said a blind animal has no inspection from outside but from inside — there is a difference for safek drusah; and Rashba wrote in his novellae in the name of Rashi: that which they mentioned safek drusah is incidentally to teach that we are concerned for safek drusah, and we learn that Rabbah holds we are concerned for safek drusah; and nevertheless for Rabbah Tosafot proved with complete proof that even with a defect from life we follow presumption — and Tosafot challenged well from Rabbah to Rava. And on this matter I resolved what Tosafot answer: drusah is common; and if so Tosafot hold presumption is presumption of the body that it is not treifah — to say it was not gored; if not common it does not help, since it is not common. If so, in Ketubot she says "I am certain" — I hold like Rabbenu Gershom that she is believed that she has presumption of the body and we remove money even though "I am certain" is not common, as explained in Rosh and Yerushalmi; and see laws of migo in Rosh brought in Shach Choshen Mishpat siman 82 — "I am certain" is not common, and nevertheless since she has presumption of the body she is believed, and there is no need for "certainly" and "perhaps" — on daf 35 it is proven from the Gemara and Tosafot that even in "perhaps" and "perhaps" we claim "I am certain" even though not common. And likewise Ketubot 77a the Gemara challenges: R' Meir concedes regarding blemishes fit to return to her father's house that the father must bring proof; and the Gemara challenges: and why — the money is in your hand; and it is difficult what is the challenge — perhaps the explanation is blemishes fit to be born in a short time while she is in her husband's house; and if so this is presumption of the body since it is not common to be afterward in her husband's house — and so it must be said: even though not common, since presumption assists we follow it. And at first glance it requires further study; but in truth my answer above suffices for practical law — Tosafot did not say definitively this explanation of not common and common; for in truth, since there is a defect from life, presumption goes away — only Tosafot wrote this for Rabbah, for according to Rabbah one must say we are concerned for safek drusah because it is common; and for us there is no difficulty from "I am certain" — it may be said they hold like the other version there that the reason is not because of their presumption but from the power of migo; and for practical law: these are Rama's words directed to the above; and Rashba also agreed as Shach wrote. And regarding a defect by human hands, etc., Be'er Heitev wrote well that every defect from life cannot be established on its presumption — it must be according to the conclusion that this in Yevamot does not stand, and one cannot challenge: if so Tosafot hold that with a defect from life we do not follow presumption — if so why did Tosafot toil in Chullin 11a s.v. atya to forbid cheeses where a treifah animal was found and we do not say establish on presumption of kashrus; and now that it became treifah it has no clear presumption — see there; and they did not write plainly in Be'er HaGolah "blow" and such as sircha that certainly we declare treifah in them that there was a defect from life — then there is no presumption; and all the more it challenges on Rama, Semak, and Or Zarua who hold that with a defect from life we do not say we follow presumption — why below siman 81 did they rule regarding cheeses even in a blow in the hoof that before three days the cheeses are permitted, for we follow presumption; and behold there is a defect from life. And one may say this is not difficult in safek yibbum or safek drusah — "you say establish on her presumption she was not divorced and not gored" — the defect that happened to her from life opposes and weakens the power of presumption; unlike presumption of the body to say establish on her presumption — presumption of the body that now deteriorated does not establish the defect in life and does not oppose them; for on this we judge whether the defect was born today or yesterday, and we say it was born today and yesterday there was no defect at all — and both are correct. And what can still be reconciled in Rama's words: for without this, in winter days I examined Tosafot's words that appear s.v. atya — they wrote: one may learn from this practical halacha that if he made cheeses from many animals and afterward one was slaughtered and found treifah — all are forbidden, and one may not say establish the cow on its presumption, etc., end of his words. And one must understand what Tosafot wanted from this — that he made from many animals and one was found treifah; in brief it should have said: if he made cheeses from an animal's milk and it was found treifah — and similarly Tosafot wrote at the beginning of Niddah, see there; and therefore it appears that certainly even according to Maharam of Lublin and his supporters who hold like Rabbah — where a defect was born from life, nevertheless we follow presumption rabbinically and certainly forbidden, and we do not establish presumption to be lenient — for Abaye and Rava hold she requires chalitzah both in kiddushin and divorce; and this is because they hold to be stringent as Beit Yosef wrote; and therefore those who are stringent — not specifically because of yibbum and chalitzah alone that is mentioned here, for the entire nature of chalitzah is only a lav — and what is this lav more than other lavin in the Torah; if so, just as they hold to be stringent on the lav of chalitzah and not rely on presumption, so too regarding all prohibitions of lavin one may be stringent thus without relying on presumption — and this is simple and correct, and as they said in two and two — a safek d'rabbanan; the rabbis were stringent without relying on presumption, and the same here; and if so, rabbinically we certainly do not rely on presumption. And do not challenge: if so what did Tosafot challenge from safek drusah — establish her presumption even though a defect was born from life — perhaps rabbinically they are forbidden; one may say on 53b we learn that Shmuel forbids safek drusah from this safek drusah that came before Shmuel and he threw it in the river; and the Gemara challenges: but this is two safekot — safek treifah safek kosher; and you will say treifah — lest it not come to a stumbling block; and they answer: the first safek is from the Torah forbidden; and if so it is proven from the Torah it is forbidden, and they challenged well; and they answer: they are common; and if so it appears clear to all that with a defect from life we do not follow presumption rabbinically and it is forbidden — and they are correct for practical law in Rama's words; and this is correct. And if so, one may say that Tosafot at the beginning of Chullin also hold thus; and if so, plainly regarding a cow that was slaughtered — if to permit its milk that was milked beforehand from presumption, Tosafot knew this is not the law, for a defect was born from life, and in any case rabbinically forbidden; and therefore Tosafot wrote that milk was taken from many animals and one of them was treifah — then from the Torah it is permitted because the majority annuls the minority in its kind, only rabbinically forbidden wet in wet in 60 in its kind; and if so, two rabbinical prohibitions we do not forbid, and there is room to permit; and therefore Tosafot came with another reason — forbidden because there is no clear presumption, etc. — and requires further study. And even though the words are comfortable in Tosafot's plain meaning, nevertheless on this I return — and the truth is as I wrote: to say establish presumption, and now that it became treifah — this "since a defect was born from life" nullifies it, for on this we judge whether the defect was born now or from days ago; but what he wrote to distinguish between Torah and rabbinic — there is room in halacha, and Rama wrote well, for so is proven from chapter Four Brothers that in any case rabbinically it is forbidden — for we see they are stringent regarding chalitzah without following presumption; and if so, everywhere in such a case we forbid rabbinically. And I did not know what this clamor is to permit where there is a defect from life that in any case is rabbinically forbidden — there is no substance in two poskim who permit; and behold it is clear to forbid. But if a defect was not clearly born — according to Tosafot's view one may permit only where it is common; I am still not clear in Tosafot's view whether they also said treifah — Tosafot spoke only of drusah and its kind where there is a defect from life; for I already wrote above proof from "I am certain" that even though not common, and likewise "I was forced" — there is a voice for ones and majority of will as Tosafot and Rosh wrote; and nevertheless presumption was rejected — and Tosafot Ketubot 36a s.v. hacheresh explicitly — not because of "certain" but because of presumption; and therefore they hold there is no distinction between common and not common; at first Tosafot intended to disagree with Rashi who leaned on deafness and "I was forced" to remove money because of presumption of the body that deteriorated; and in the end they conceded and brought proof from a needle found in the synagogue — the seller must lose and we remove money from the butcher-buyer's hand immediately, and he cannot say "in your possession it became treifah" — they established on its presumption. And it is difficult what proof is there that it is common — whether before sale or after sale; but ones and "I am certain" are not common — only we learn as I wrote that even though not common, presumption is strong. And that which they answered regarding drusah — because it is common — apart from what I wrote above, one may also say: since there are two factors — a defect born from life and common — we declare treifah; but where a defect was not born from life, presumption is strong and overrides even though treifot are common. Nevertheless it appears to be stringent where common; but where treifah and kashrus are equal — I already wrote clearly from Tosafot's words that we go leniently and rely on presumption of kashrus; and so Rama wrote explicitly regarding a matter where we can say the defect was done — we say "she was slaughtered, she is permitted." But Shach and Peri Chadash brought Rashba and Ran in the name of Rabbenu Yonah who ruled: every safek in slaughter — not specifically; the same in everything where there are no legs that it was done after slaughter — we hang stringently, etc., as Semak and Or Zarua wrote. And it must be said: that which we say before slaughter it is in a presumption of prohibition and after slaughter in a presumption of permissibility — this is to require inspection; in slaughter one must check afterward in signs and knife, unlike other types of treifot that have a presumption of permissibility (and it is not difficult — this is not from the law of presumption but from the law of majority, as we derive from slaughter itself that we follow majority; one may say safek whether he slaughtered as needed or a knife without defect — this is b'ydei adam and we do not follow majority — requires further study). And I am crude and do not know expert animals of permissibility — an animal from which cheeses were made from its milk and it was found treifah: we come to judge the milk and cheeses per Rashba and Ran — if there is room to say the treifah occurred immediately before slaughter we permit the cheeses; and all the more if there is room to say it was done only after slaughter, per Rashba in guarding the house that there must be safek perhaps done after slaughter — then the cheeses are permitted; and see below siman 61 — Taz disagrees, as written below; the correct view is with him; and according to all, if there is safek whether treifot were done after slaughter we permit the cheeses and you forbid the animal and attribute to it a Torah safek. And it is possible to know guarding the house regarding milk there is a double safek — perhaps it is not treifah at all, for after slaughter it happened; and you will say before slaughter — perhaps after it was milked; but in the animal there is only one safek — this is strained, for the entire section of guarding the house is there on daf 87 because of presumption and R' Shimon's view that presumption is from majority and not from double safek — and this answers what is written in guarding the house: one may say after slaughter — if so remove presumption from here; behold there is double safek (and even though it does not reverse, we do not need it, as written below siman 110, see there); and also as appears from Rashba's novellae the explanation is immediately before slaughter and not after slaughter; and all the more it challenges Semak and Or Zarua who copied Rama below siman 81 in their words regarding a blow in the hoof — certainly within three days it became treifah, but before three days the milk is permitted; behold there is only safek whether before three days or afterward, and the animal is certainly treifah and they permit the milk and establish on presumption of kashrus; and if the safek is in the animal whether before slaughter or after slaughter to declare treifah because of Torah safek — this matter is far from sense. And Rashba himself regarding a defect by hand hung the safek to prohibit because a defect was born from life; and behold even though he wrote as above in the name of R' Yonah regarding "she was slaughtered, she is permitted" that the animal stands in a presumption of prohibition — he wrote explicitly that if a safek of treifot was born, everything we can hang after slaughter we establish for it presumption of permissibility, for presumptively animals are not treifot, end of his words — requires further study. And therefore it is clear they spoke only regarding a matter far in reality that it was done after slaughter — such as the wing broke before slaughter, which is common that a bird flies; but after slaughter it rests in its place — who will break it; and therefore we do not hang. And in truth Rashba wrote: if it is possible to hang that they trampled it — in truth kosher; and so all; but if it is a balanced safek and there is no defect from life here — all permit per Rama in Shulchan Aruch, and one may not budge from his ruling whether to be stringent or lenient. Only I need to learn: since Tosafot forbid cheeses because there is no clear presumption and they do not hold like Rashi that presumption is from majority — what is my difficulty from safek drusah; behold there is no clear presumption here. And it is possible Tosafot did not intend presumption of returned kashrus that is not clear — perhaps there is some treifah in the animal; but Tosafot intended presumption of the body — let us say establish presumption it was not gored; and if so this is regarding safek drusah — clear presumption that when it was gored at birth it crouched under its mother; but this is strained — if so regarding safek in slaughter where we say establish on presumption of prohibition, let us say establish on presumption of the body that when born it was dislocated or uprooted, etc.; and likewise regarding cheeses with healthy sircha that we declare treifah in them, or a needle in the synagogue across and across, and a blow in the hoof — let us say establish presumption it was not born with a needle and blow in the hoof — requires further study from the womb; and therefore it must be said: what of it — nevertheless it could be that without this it would be perforated from the womb; and so too regarding drusah one may say the reason for treifah of goring is that goring perforates the intestinal children, and perhaps it was perforated from the womb; and if treifah is found in the kidney that shrank, one may permit the milk from clear presumption — if born from the womb kosher even though it shrank; and behold here there is clear presumption; only since there is no clarity in all treifot — what difference treifah elsewhere or in this organ — nevertheless it is not clear that it is kosher; and if so, likewise regarding drusah. And therefore it appears that certainly Tosafot who declared the cheeses treifah — even though we hold we follow majority and most animals are kosher, as Maharshal wrote — it must be said as Maharshal wrote in Yesh Nochalin: most animals do not have a defect. And with this one may answer that which we say "she was slaughtered, she is permitted" — a wolf came and took the intestinal children and returned them perforated — we hang on the wolf because of presumption; behold this is not clear presumption that perhaps it was perforated from life; but according to the above it is fine: for most animals are not perforated, and here there is no defect, and we say it was not perforated at all; and the presumption the Gemara mentions is not specifically the main reason is because of majority; and therefore also regarding safek drusah one may say we follow presumption and majority, and it does not appear at all as Tosafot wrote "she was slaughtered, she is permitted" — and this is because of majority, and the same here; and therefore Tosafot answered well that drusah is common — and what will the majority help, behold most animals gore and drusah is common; and with this what I wrote regarding presumption is resolved — even regarding a common matter, for here the main matter is from majority, and this came and nullified that. But with all this my heart is not at ease, for Tosafot's language instructs everything on presumption and not on majority; and one whose mind is broad should set his heart to reconcile Tosafot's words on the clear and receive reward from the Giver of the Torah. And it is possible the essence of Tosafot's question from majority is only: do not say since there is a defect here we do not follow majority, for the majority does not have this defect as a deed — there was water in the head and most animals do not need water in the head; and on this Tosafot brought from presumption: just as this defect from life does not harm presumption, so it does not harm majority; and if so the language implies presumption — it is strained and I establish myself. And Maharsha explained thus in Tosafot's words — Tosafot challenged Rava who holds regarding perforation the esophagus has inspection — what does he challenge, and R' Meir how did he eat meat lest he checked; Maharsha challenged — lest he was concerned for drusah he should have refrained from eating meat that has no inspection; and at first glance his words require further study — behold Tosafot explained we are concerned for safek drusah because they are common — and this is in an animal we see it was silent, etc.; but in animals in general, even if common that they are gored — if so you have not left an animal for the shepherd of the flock; and if so we follow presumption; and according to what I wrote it is fine — all Tosafot's challenges are because of majority, and the challenge to R' Meir is that he did not follow majority. And likewise Rambam who wrote: since drusah is explicit in the Torah, every safek one doubts in it — behold it is forbidden; and to resolve Beit Yosef's challenge — if safek is permitted in other prohibitions it is forbidden here — one may say as Taz wrote at the beginning of siman 29: Rambam's intent is Tosafot's challenge — why say safek drusah is forbidden and we do not establish presumption; and therefore Rambam holds the reason is that it is explicit from the Torah; and that which the Gemara challenges Ula in "a thorn sat in the esophagus" from drusah — even though this is explicit and that is not explicit — there is no difficulty, for an incident occurred that the esophagus was perforated — it is nevelah, and explicit nevelah is in the category of nevelah, and the matter of the generality of treifot does not apply; and with this the Rosh's challenge from a needle in the synagogue is resolved — a needle was not explicit in the Torah like this in siman 33; and there is more to explain: what of it that it is explicit in the Torah or comes in Hilchot Melachim — why should there be a difference to forbid its safek; but regarding the essence of the reason we are concerned for safek drusah because it is common — only who testifies it is common? Only the Torah testified that it chose this treifah to explain more than the other eighteen treifot — and this is because it is prevalent and common; and if so, since the Torah testified it is common, it is simple one must forbid its safek — examine well. And if so, according to Rambam's words it is simple it is permitted if they are equal, per his view in all treifot — and requires further study:`,
    '2#_': `And in great loss one may rely on those who permit. Behold the Rav in Bach siman 54 wondered — above in siman 43 the Mechaber and Rama left undifferentiated that with two livers it is treifah — and this is Rashba's view, for according to Rambam it is kosher; and how did the Mechaber bring here Rambam's view too, and Rama wrote one may rely on him in great loss. And behold in truth in Rambam himself chapter 6 of Hilchot Shechitah din 20, who wrote: the spleen was removed or two spleens were found — kosher; and he did not write two livers — it implies he holds like Hagahot Gedolot that it is treifah; however from this there is no proof that he holds perforation of ducts within the liver is treifah; and if so, the same applies to liver deficiency — certainly the ducts were ruined, and therefore treifah; and one cannot challenge from Hagahot Gedolot's law. But for us, who hold not like them and perforation does not disqualify ducts — nevertheless we declare treifah with two livers, which implies not like Rambam; however, according to what is written in Ra'avad siman 43, one may say that in any case Ran decides not like Rambam — from that which is taught "all excess is considered as removed" and it is not taught "as deficient"; if there were a difference between removed and deficient, see there; and according to his words it must be said: even for Rashba it is difficult why it teaches "as removed" and not "as deficient"; and it must be said it is mere language, since there is no practical difference — and this did not please Rambam's eyes; therefore he holds on the contrary there is a difference between created missing and removed. However, the Sages had a tradition that in excess thus weakness prevails in the animal as if the organ were removed — for weakness is stronger than created missing; so excess of the weak organ prevails until it cannot endure and both become useless and go and are considered removed through weakness; and this is what he inferred in explaining their words: "all excess" — not like created missing, only like removed; for through excess that organ eventually becomes entirely gone and going and becoming deficient — and therefore it says specifically "as removed." And if so, this case of two livers that is treifah is for all; but the Rav in Tur HaShalem, according to what is written below siman 54, implies he does not hold thus — and see what I wrote there, for in my humble opinion it is clear as I wrote. And behold at first glance there is proof for Rashba who argues on Rambam from the Gemara Niddah 24a: sealed body does not become impure through childbirth — and what is a sealed body? Rabbi says: enough that one take from the living and it dies; R' Zekharyah says: until the knee; R' Yanai says: until its navel; R' Yosi says in the name of R' Yosi bar Yehoshua: until the place of its navel. And the Gemara says: between R' Zekharyah and R' Yosi there is a dispute whether treifah lives — some say treifah lives and some say treifah does not live; and Rashi explained: if cut from the knee upward — treifah; and he holds treifah does not live; and R' Yanai disagrees and holds treifah lives; and between R' Yosi and Yochanan there is a difference — that R' Eliezer says the thigh was removed and its cavity, and Rashi explained: R' Yochanan does not have R' Eliezer, etc., see there. And at first glance it is difficult — behold we taught "I gave you legs from the knee and upward," etc.; but deficient we did not teach, only cut — namely removed; and if so, created missing according to Rambam is kosher — and behold there the body is sealed, which is created missing; and if so kosher according to Rambam; and in the Gemara it implies for all treifah — only the disputant holds treifah lives; and this is at first glance proof for Rashba's words. But in truth one may say Rambam concedes regarding deficient leg it is treifah. However, after examination I saw that this is exactly one of the proofs Rambam had in this law — Rambam ruled in Hilchot Issurei Bi'ah chapter 10 halacha 11: sealed body that does not become impure through childbirth — this is until its navel per R' Yosi bar Yehoshua; and they already wondered on him — R' Yosi bar Yehoshua holds thigh and cavity removed is not nevelah and also treifah lives; but according to what he ruled that thigh and cavity removed is nevelah — if so he should have ruled until its navel; and the matter requires further study. At first glance, this that he ruled treifah does not live — there is no difficulty; one may say he holds like Rashba's novellae in the name of Ramban: since it lives, the offspring is viable; and it must be said that which we say "treifah does not live" there in Niddah is per R' Meir there in Chullin who does not live more than a novelty; but this is difficult. And therefore it appears it is difficult for Rambam — if so the tannaim argue on that of R' Eliezer who said the thigh was removed, etc.; why does the Gemara challenge in Chullin 32b and consider also that R' Eliezer's thigh and cavity were removed — nevelah; and it is difficult to establish the mishnah like R' Yosi bar Yehoshua who argues on R' Eliezer, and the anonymous mishnah like R' Yosi bar Yehoshua who is a tanna; and similarly the Gemara answers in chapter 8 as many tannaim, see there; therefore Rambam holds they did not argue on the body of treifot, only on this reasoning whether created missing and removal are one law, whether there are offspring in them; and therefore R' Yosi who said until its navel holds: just as removal is treifah or nevelah, so created missing; but R' Yosi bar Yehoshua holds: in truth it is nevelah, only this is in removal; but in created missing — treifah and not nevelah; and all these are created missing and there is no nevelah here; and therefore we need until the navel; and therefore the Gemara in Chullin cited cannot establish that mishnah — those treifot of R' Yosi bar Yehoshua who argues on R' Eliezer — for on the essence of the law in removal they do not argue, only argue on created missing like sealed body; and Rambam who decides this reasoning between created missing and removal — therefore ruled like R' Yosi bar Yehoshua; and one support for his friends — and the matters are clear — examine. And so is proven from Tosafot 57a s.v. dilema, etc., who wrote: and this should be said — even nevelah too is called if the thigh and its cavity were removed, as R' Eliezer said, and there is no one who disagrees; and so they wrote in the statement before it: why, etc., that it is not omitted to teach in one place dislocated thigh in an animal kosher — there is R' Yosi bar Yehoshua who does not have it; and therefore entirely kosher he holds; if treifah — R' Yanai holds treifah does not live; and if you will say R' Yanai and R' Yosi bar Yehoshua argue in two matters in treifah whether it lives and in R' Eliezer thigh removed, etc. — this is not the sugya of the Gemara. At first glance, from Tamid 11b where we say: R' does not have R' Eliezer who said removed, etc. — there is no difficulty for Tosafot — there one may say we do not need R' Eliezer who said nevelah, but treifah nevertheless; but here it is proven he holds entirely kosher — difficult; and therefore it must be said Tosafot hold like Rambam — strained to say they argue on the essence of the law that we do not find, etc., only if deficient in the category; but removed — certainly treifah; and the matters stand as they are, for Tosafot and Rambam agree as one — and who will open his mouth against them; and therefore, since Rambam has a good peg, Rama wrote one may rely in great loss; and so is the view of Tur HaShalem and Or Zarua; and especially where there is otherwise a branch to lean on; and even Peri Chadash who is stringent — clear:`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `A place where one cannot. See Shach; and see in responsa Beit Yaakov siman 84 and in responsa Noda BiYehudah part 2 Yoreh De'ah siman 8 and in responsa Toldot Yitzchak siman 18 [and see in responsa Chemdat Shlomo 62 what he wrote on this at length]:`,
    '2#_': `[To created missing. See Be'er Heitev what he wrote: a goose whose skin was whole, etc., until: but because it was a new matter not seen like it by the multitude of treifah inspectors. And see in responsa Shevut Yaakov part 3 siman 62 that a similar question came before him and he permitted too; and wrote that Tzitzat Tzvi declared treifah because it was a new matter and some were swayed by the matter; but for the law it appears to me it is kosher; and if another such case is found and comes before us we will permit it; and all the more in this case where it appears (more) that the board was missing from the beginning of its creation — and especially since the owner of the goose told me that in all the geese there was no fat like this, and if it were treifah it would have withered, see there]:`,
    '3#_': `Those who permit in this. See in responsa Noda BiYehudah part 2 Yoreh De'ah siman 5 in the middle of the responsum where he was in doubt regarding Rambam's view (who is among those who permit this) — what about broken organs, namely an organ whose treifah is in the category of broken — if created thus whether it is treifah — see there at length:`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 50 in the hagahah seif 1) Every place where one can, etc. Shach wrote s.k. 3: an incident came before us in Krakow, etc. — before coming to explain his words we precede Rashba's words in his novellae first chapter of Chullin 4 s.v. velitei, etc.: and where the bird's wing slipped off, etc. — our Rabbi wrote it is reasonable that this is a Torah safek and stringently; and as is taught, etc.; and the same applies to all other treifot; and as we say below daf 51a regarding that statement of sending the leg after the other — R' Yema said: this is Shegruna; Ravina challenged him: perhaps the spinal cord was severed; he said to him: Shegruna is common — we learn: wherever a defect was born, if it is not because it is common — we hang stringently; only where there is something to hang, such as that which we hang on a wolf — we hang leniently, end of Rashba in his novellae in the name of his teacher. One who examines Darkei Torah chapter 8 daf 43 s.v. k'savar Ula, etc., and likewise Yevamot 30b s.v. deha isha zo, etc. — will see he holds: not only where one can say it was done after slaughter do we hang leniently, but even if the safek is from life — such as a thorn sat in the esophagus that certainly was inserted while alive, only the safek is whether it perforated outward or not — we also hang leniently, see there. And behold according to Tosafot's method it is difficult — in truth from Shegruna that is common, and likewise from that which he slaughtered the esophagus, etc. — and according to the flow of the matter one may reject that of Shegruna — for he wants to resolve the difficulty of the matter: Shegruna is common; and the same if it were not common we would hang leniently; and see above that if it were not common, inspection would in any case be needed — wherever inspection is possible we do not rely on presumption, unlike if Shegruna is common — inspection is not needed; and likewise that which he slaughtered the esophagus must be according to Tosafot's method: since the safek is in slaughter it is more severe and we do not establish presumption; and it appears Maharshal explains Tosafot's method according to Rama — only Taz himself adds and says there: where inspection is needed, if it cannot be inspected or was lost — treifah, see there; only Rama's language here does not bear this explanation that he wrote "even though it is treifah that has inspection" — it implies not only treifah that cannot be inspected do we not establish presumption. And according to Taz's view — specifically treifah that has inspection do we not establish presumption, but treifah that has no inspection we properly establish presumption. And what Shach wants to say that Tosafot wrote thus for Rabbah, etc. — wondrous, for from Tosafot's words it is proven that this reasoning of establishing presumption applies also to the conclusion; otherwise what does Tosafot challenge from Rabbah who said "she was slaughtered, she is permitted," etc. — lest Rav Huna holds like Abaye; and are you comparing man to man. Also in Tosafot chapter 8 daf 43 it implies explicitly that that which they challenge there — why not say we establish presumption — from the outset we hold thus; therefore it appears to me that one may say in Tosafot Yevamot there they resolve with drusah is common — if so it is difficult for Rav Huna to enumerate the eighteen treifot born from life and not common, such as water found in the head and we do not know if the brain surrounded it — we also say "she was slaughtered, she is permitted"; and likewise that which is stated "a thorn sat in the esophagus," etc. — wondrous why Tosafot did not mention this in their challenge and in their answer why they took what they did not challenge from the outset. And one may answer: behold Tosafot with Rabbenu Shimshon disagree regarding cheese that was made and afterward a treifah animal was found — Tosafot hold the cheeses are forbidden and we do not say establish presumption; and now that it became treifah — because presumption is not clear, for from the day it was born one may say now it became treifah; and Rabbenu Shimshon holds the cheeses are permitted — we say one hour before slaughter it became treifah, we establish presumption, and it is clear presumption because of majority; and see Tosafot Chullin 11a s.v. atya from a red cow, etc., and their dispute: Tosafot hold since now there is a defect, the animal is not from majority — thus its majority deteriorated also retroactively and it is not presumption from majority; and Rabbenu Shimshon holds: now it deteriorated for it; but retroactively there was no defect, and we say presumptively it was from majority — if so, according to Tosafot it is difficult that which Rabbah said "she was slaughtered, she is permitted" — we hang on a wolf because we establish presumption; behold this is presumption that is not clear. And it must be said: since there is room to hang on a wolf, there is no defect here at all and its majority did not deteriorate, and it is presumption from majority; and one cannot challenge: if so in any case why does Rabbah take the reason of presumption that is from majority — let him say from majority. One may say: because he came to inform us where a safek was born that has majority for prohibition against the majority that permitted — Rabbah teaches us that nevertheless it is kosher because of presumption. If so, according to the flow Tosafot challenge nothing — why did Rabbah not also say safek drusah; and likewise that which they challenge in Chullin to establish safek drusah on presumption from life — behold there is no clear presumption here. And it must be said they hold: granted, regarding other treifot there is a defect and it is not presumption from majority, only regarding drusah this certainly was not at the hour it was born, for all the while it did not see a lion enter — even though the flesh opposite the intestinal children reddened — nevertheless kosher; if so there is fine clear presumption from the outset. And Tosafot challenge well from safek drusah; according to this it is fine why they challenge: why did Rabbah not enumerate other eighteen treifot where safek was born from life — for regarding other treifot there is no clear presumption here. Only this is difficult for them: why did they not take safek drusah that has clear presumption; and they answered drusah is common. And that which they wrote "and likewise a thorn sat," etc. — not that Tosafot want to answer what Rabbah did not mention in his words that of the thorn — on that they would not challenge from the outset, for it is different there because there was no clear presumption; only this is difficult for them: according to what they want to answer that drusah is common — if so what does the Gemara challenge at the beginning of chapter 8, and what is different from safek drusah — drusah is common; and on this they wrote: nevertheless it challenges well, for also "a thorn sat in the esophagus" is common to perforate; and accordingly Rama wrote here the hagahah: every safek born from life — there is no establishing presumption, for it is presumption that is not clear; according to this Darkei Moshe's words are correct that so is implied in Tosafot chapter Four Brothers — because they are strained in answering that drusah is common, nevertheless it is difficult why they did not take other safek treifot born from life that are not common; and it must be said it is presumption that is not clear, as I wrote — requires further study. But it is difficult — certainly Tosafot hold thus, because according to their method they hold that every other safek treifah is presumption that is not clear; but according to Rama below siman 81 seif 2 regarding a blow in the hoof — one may not forbid the milk milked from it before three days; and if so, perforce they hold like Rabbenu Shimshon's method that it is presumption from majority — see there in Shach s.k. 4. And if so Rama's words contradict each other; and one may say: this case of cheese — even according to those who argue on Rabbenu Shimshon it is permitted, for the entire reason of Tosafot is where there is a defect the presumption deteriorated; but here, since the safek depends on time, we say now it deteriorated and regarding before that there was no defect at all — unlike here with water in the brain where the safek is whether the brain surrounded it — we would have to establish presumption that the brain surrounded it; we say well: since there is a defect, presumption deteriorated since now in any case there is a defect; therefore Rashba and Tosafot forbid even though they do not forbid for one reason alone — Rama did not want to be lenient against them; and see above. And behold it is difficult for Tosafot's method who establish presumption from the outset — difficult is that which they answer Shegruna is common, as Rashba brought his proof; and therefore it must be said one of two reasons, either because it is presumption that is not clear; and according to Rabbenu Shimshon who holds it is clear presumption — in truth it must be like Rashba's method; if so here in any case it is forbidden; but below he holds Rabbenu Shimshon's method is primary, and since retroactively there was no defect the cheeses are permitted. And that which they took Shegruna is common — this means inspection is not even needed; this does not appear to Rama; therefore he ruled here to prohibit, and even where inspection is impossible — nevertheless the sugya of "a thorn sat in the esophagus" that Rosh ruled in the name of Ra'avan like Ula and brings proof from a needle found in the thickness of beit hakosot — see there; if so, perforce he does not hold like Rashba's method, but holds even where there is a defect we establish presumption; if so it is difficult for his method that Shegruna is common, and also from that which he slaughtered the esophagus and the windpipe was found dislocated — he said it is presumption that is not clear, as I wrote for Tosafot's method — behold Rosh holds like Rabbenu Shimshon's method that it is presumption from majority; therefore it must be said that which they took Shegruna is common — this means inspection is even needed; and that which he slaughtered the esophagus, etc. — this is because it was severed in slaughter which is more severe than other safek treifot; if so, perforation of the esophagus is safek in slaughter — for if the esophagus is perforated its slaughter does not purify it from nevelah at all, as written siman 33 seif 3. According to this it is difficult: what proof does he bring from a found needle, etc. — behold perforation of the esophagus is more severe, for it is safek in slaughter; and it must be said his proof is thus: since the found needle does not need even inspection from one side — if so, perforce it must be said it was perforated from one side, it does not enter the category of safek at all; if so, the same here — kosher even though it is safek in slaughter, for it does not enter the category of safek; and according to this it is proven not like Bach that I brought above siman 48 seif 7 who explains that l'chatchila inspection is needed in any case — see there that if so it is safek in slaughter — examine well:`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `(1) [Seif 1] For all these perforations, if there is something to hang upon — such as the butcher or a wolf that took them — we hang upon them. And see above siman 36 seif 5 regarding the laws of butcher's hands and wolf, and in our words there at length.`,
    '2#_': `(2) There. And if there is nothing to hang upon and doubt whether it was perforated from life or not — we pierce a perforation next to it and compare, etc. Hagahah: and see above siman 36 how we practice. Meaning that there he wrote in the hagahah at the end of seif 5 that in our time we are not expert and one may not rely on hakafah at all to permit, end of his words; and see in our words to Shaha ot 114 and ot 115 where we wrote that such is the view of all acharonim that nowadays one may not rely on hakafah at all — see there.`,
    '3#_': `(3) And regarding the incident where when they opened the animal's paunch a large spurt of murky water jumped on them — Peri Chadash brought ot 1 and other acharonim already wrote this above siman 39 ot 164 — we took from there.`,
  },
  'yad-avraham': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 50 seif 1 in the hagahah) Every place where one cannot hang that it was done after slaughter — even though it has inspection, if they did not inspect it or cannot inspect it — behold it is like safek treifah and we forbid it. And he wrote in responsa Tur HaEven siman 41 that even the hagahah was stringent only if it was already slaughtered and the organ was lost so it cannot be inspected; but when it was not yet slaughtered he admits we go leniently — and therefore he permits slaughtering on Yom Tov a bird in which a defect was born. And therefore he permits milk from an animal that limps and it is not known if its nivin were eaten. And especially in this treifah that certainly was not born with it and it has presumption of its body — also Tur HaShalem is lenient. And moreover R' Dain: we do not establish from prohibition to prohibition in safek of reality from Yevamot (daf 119) — we do not learn presumption of a married woman from presumption of prohibition of a yevamah for the marketplace. And therefore only regarding throwing a get are we stringent because Tosafot wrote in Ketubot (daf 22) that we have a get before us — unlike a balanced treifot book where even though a defect was born from life that was in a presumption that it is not slaughtered — it is a different prohibition. And all the more there is a majority of kashrus and no other majority against it to remove it. And even in a book if the esophagus was perforated — nevertheless "she was slaughtered" before us. Only in a book where it sat or was gored we establish from life that it is one prohibition that does not leave, and also there is in it "I am certain" — organ 45 like intestinal children when it was not slaughtered properly — as one who places in a basket; from when it was slaughtered siman 1 and they did not permit unless it was slaughtered with slaughter that permits to Israel. And in siman 17 he agreed to Tzitzat Tzvi's view regarding a found needle — we hang that it fell; and the same regarding found broken — if it is not recognizable that it was done from life we establish the original presumption and now it deteriorated — and not like Tur HaShalem. And see in Levushei Serad who wrote plainly to forbid lost buka without inspection, and he saw this from Mordechai forbidding without inspection. And there is no proof — Mordechai is according to his view who holds like the poskim Shach brings that we do not say "she was slaughtered, she is permitted" where there is nothing to hang on for permissibility, as Tur HaShalem wrote siman 29. But according to what Shach concludes to permit in great loss — likewise permitted:`,
    '2#_': `(Seif 2) And the meaning of what we said: if created missing — treifah, all the more if removed by hand; and everything we said: if removed — treifah, the same law applies to created missing; and some permit. This is Rambam. And in my humble opinion he deals with treifot that became treifah because of themselves — such as the liver was removed or flayed, which is in the category of removed, as Rashi wrote beginning of chapter 8 (daf 43) and Shach brings siman 29. But in treifah that is not because of itself — such as the upper cheek was removed, which Rambam declares treifah as written siman 33 seif 2 because of lung cooling. And if so, there is no distinction here between removed by hand or created missing — for if the cheek was created missing, this is lung deficiency that we taught in the mishnah in chapter 8, or that it was deficient — as above, clear. And with this the Rashba's challenge in Tur HaShalem (daf 43) on Rambam is removed. And see at length siman 41. And what Tur HaShalem wrote on Rambam at the end of siman 29 from the Gemara Niddah (daf 24) — he left in requires further study; also the Rav in Kiryat Sefer sensed this and they settled it — see there. Indeed, what he wrote that there is proof for Rambam from the above Gemara — in my humble opinion one may distinguish between person and animal, as written in novellae on Niddah and in Rambam's composition Hilchot Issurei Bi'ah. And accordingly one may also reject what he brought as proof from Tosafot chapter 8 (daf 57) who hold like Rambam:`,
  },
  'yad-ephraim': {
    '1#_': `All these perforations. See Peri Chadash and Tur HaShalem in what he wrote in the name of Penei Moshe: when water is found in the body cavity; and see in Noda BiYehudah part 1 siman 25: when water is found in the body cavity one must inspect the internal organs, etc.; therefore one must be stringent even in great loss. And in responsa Bach siman 157 regarding a chicken whose belly was swollen, etc., and it was copied in Likkutei HaKabbala; and see in Shevut Yaakov part 2 siman 65 where he brought Bach's words and wrote: thus in water found in the body cavity in a chicken — in Bach's responsum siman 157 he ruled to permit when no defect is seen and there is room to hang on illness as is the way of all swollen ones that have water between skin and flesh; and one who wishes to be stringent may be stringent for himself and not for others. And see in my books Bach siman 39; and see in Peri Chadash regarding an animal similarly — he concludes one may not permit except in great loss; apart from that I heard from physician-sages that there is danger in eating such food — one must be concerned it will also lead to illness called (wassersucht); and similarly I heard regarding chickens whose legs are swollen — even in the lower bone one must be concerned it will lead to illness podagra, meaning gout; and those who eat come to this illness — and the matter is common in chickens; therefore one who guards his soul should distance himself from them:`,
  },
};
